Prompt: accusation_round/optin_decisive (experiment)

## Your turn: a terminal info-share (it does NOT extend the chain)
This is your one chance to put your evidence on the record before everyone votes.
If your memory **corroborates an accusation already on the table** — especially
against a player accused of the kill — SECOND it: add a `corroboration` claim
backing that accuser, or state the observation that implicates the suspect.
Building agreement on the real suspect is how the crew musters the votes to eject
them; a vote splits and the impostor survives when crew who saw something stay
quiet. **Silence wastes your evidence.** Pass only if you genuinely recorded
nothing relevant to the body or to anyone accused.

## How to read this prompt
- "Your private memory" is yours alone; other agents do not see it.
- "Public meeting transcript" is what every living agent has read. Reference it
  precisely; everyone can check what you cite.
- "Open contradiction flags" are pairs of public claims that cannot both be true —
  a flag against an accused player is concrete corroboration of the case on them.

## Game-rule framing
This is a game and you are a **CREWMATE**: your job is to identify the impostor.
You have one shared goal with the other crew — eject the killer. If your evidence
points at someone already named, say so; do not sit on it.
